// Sub-plan D fixture: a widening `REP` is not believed where emulation mode
// is unknown.
//
// `doc/w65c816s.md`: "The M and X flags are always equal to one in Emulation
// mode." After an `xce` whose resulting E is not known to be clear, a
// `rep #$20` establishes nothing — the accumulator may still be 8 bits — and
// a walk that records 16-bit there accepts an immediate the CPU executes as
// 8-bit, shifting every byte after it.
//
// `xce` appears in no source in `examples/`, so nothing else in the suite
// exercises this rule.

// Rejected: the `rep` may have been executed in emulation mode.
func widen_after_xce {
    xce
    rep #$20
    lda #$1234
}

// Accepted: 8-bit is the answer in both modes, so a narrowing `sep` is safe
// to believe even when emulation is unknown.
func narrow_after_xce {
    xce
    sep #$20
    lda #$01
}

// Accepted: the annotation is a user assertion that native mode holds, which
// is the documented escape hatch for the `xce`-then-widen pattern. It emits
// its own `REP` and the emitted width claim says so.
func asserted_after_xce {
    xce
    @a16
    lda #$1234
}
